# SRG-APP-000001-WSR-000001  -  CAT II
# The web server must limit the number of allowed simultaneous session
# requests.
#
# Generated from the benchmark and the assessment determinations, then kept
# by hand. Edit this file to change how this requirement is assessed here;
# it is read at run time. Regenerating refreshes the benchmark text in place
# and leaves the criteria, the determination and any verification block as
# they are.

id:              SRG-APP-000001-WSR-000001
group:           V-206350
severity:        medium
cat:             CAT II
determination:   APPLICABLE
grade:           C
decidable:       machine
confidence:      documented
cci:             CCI-000054
control_rev5:    AC-10

contribution:    Splunk Web and the splunkd HTTP server each accept an explicit cap on
                 simultaneous HTTP connections and on active HTTP transactions, so the
                 application can bound concurrent session requests instead of leaving the
                 ceiling to automatic sizing (value 0) or to no limit at all (a negative
                 value); server.thread_pool and server.socket_timeout further bound how
                 many worker threads are held and how long an accepted connection may hold
                 a socket.

governs:         server.conf - Core server settings, including the certificates and protocols used to
                 protect management and replication traffic.
governs:         web.conf - Configures the Splunk Web interface.

check:           web.conf [settings] maxSockets min 0
check:           web.conf [settings] maxThreads min 0
check:           server.conf [httpServer] maxSockets min 0
check:           server.conf [httpServer] maxThreads min 0
# --- vendor verification: generated, do not edit by hand
corrected:       web.conf [settings] maxSockets present -> web.conf [settings] maxSockets min 0
corrected:       web.conf [settings] maxThreads present -> web.conf [settings] maxThreads min 0
corrected:       server.conf [httpServer] maxSockets min 1 -> server.conf [httpServer] maxSockets min 0
corrected:       server.conf [httpServer] maxThreads min 1 -> server.conf [httpServer] maxThreads min 0
vendor:          server.conf [httpServer] maxSockets https://help.splunk.com/en/data-management/splunk-enterprise-admin-manual/10.4/configuration-file-reference/10.4.0-configuration-file-reference/server.conf
vendor:          server.conf [httpServer] maxThreads https://help.splunk.com/en/data-management/splunk-enterprise-admin-manual/10.4/configuration-file-reference/10.4.0-configuration-file-reference/server.conf
vendor:          web.conf [settings] maxSockets https://help.splunk.com/en/splunk-enterprise/administer/admin-manual/10.4/configuration-file-reference/10.4.0-configuration-file-reference/web.conf
vendor:          web.conf [settings] maxThreads https://help.splunk.com/en/splunk-enterprise/administer/admin-manual/10.4/configuration-file-reference/10.4.0-configuration-file-reference/web.conf
# --- end vendor verification

external:        A per-user session cap, as distinct from a per-listener connection cap, is
                 not expressible in Splunk configuration; where the organization requires
                 one it is carried by the reverse proxy, load balancer, or web application
                 firewall in front of Splunk Web.
external:        The operating system's file descriptor and thread ceilings bound these
                 values further and are set outside the application (the spec notes splunkd
                 derives an automatic limit from the host's maximum allowable open files).
external:        The numeric value of the cap is DoD- and organization-defined; the
                 application's share is that a positive, explicit limit is expressed.

citation:        splunk-spec-files/web.conf.spec:1098-1112 (maxThreads, maxSockets: default
                 0 = automatic limit, negative = no limits enforced);
                 splunk-spec-files/server.conf.spec:1291 ([httpServer]), 1379-1399
                 (maxSockets, maxThreads, same semantics);
                 splunk-spec-files/web.conf.spec:835-841 (server.thread_pool, default 50,
                 maximum 200), 853-856 (server.socket_timeout, default 10);
                 SRG-APP-000001-WSR-000001 check and fix text.
finding:         Splunk Enterprise does not express an explicit positive limit on
                 simultaneous session requests, because web.conf [settings]
                 maxSockets/maxThreads or server.conf [httpServer] maxSockets/maxThreads
                 are unset, left at the automatic value 0, or set to a negative value that
                 enforces no limit; this is assessed at the application layer alone, and
                 any per-user session cap enforced by a fronting proxy is a separate matter
                 recorded as an external dependency.
